# ==== T-FMT-C · inline suite, MULTI-LINE child, header trailing comment
#
# AXIS: inline-suite header comment × MULTI-LINE child body — the cell where
# the header's own output line is NOT the buffer's last line by the time the
# comment is emitted.
#
# A header's trailing-comment hook naturally fires right after the `:`, which
# on an inline suite would put the comment BEFORE the statement and stop the
# file parsing. So the comment is CLAIMED at the header — that is what stops
# the nested match-arm loop's leading hook from pulling it down into the arm
# list, where it would document the wrong thing — and EMITTED once the body
# has rendered.
#
# WHERE it is emitted is the point of this cell. The claim records the
# header's own position and the emission resolves it to the END OF THAT LINE,
# so the comment lands where the author wrote it. Splicing at the buffer's
# then-current tail instead put it on the BODY's last line — silently, since
# the moved form reparses and is itself a fixpoint.
#
# This cell is a FIXPOINT: `gg fmt` reproduces it byte for byte. (It carried a
# `.expected` companion for as long as the comment moved.)
#
# RED procedure: resolve the claim anchor to the buffer's current tail instead
# of the recorded header line — the `# note` reappears after `30`.

void main():
    int y = 2
    if true: int x = match y:    # note
        case 2:
            20
        else:
            30
    print(1)
